    "narrative": "The Austin Waves enter today\u2019s slate with high-ceiling opportunities, anchored by the 75.27 efficiency score of **Jackson Merrill**. Facing Yesavage, **Jackson Merrill** benefits from a strong park factor and a favorable platoon matchup that projects him for a multi-hit potential, bolstered further by his slotting in the two-hole. Similarly, **Gunnar Henderson** (69.90) and **Kyle Tucker** (69.76) serve as our primary offensive engines. While **Gunnar Henderson** faces a left-handed pitcher in Cameron, his elite park factor and favorable batting order position outweigh the platoon disadvantage. **Kyle Tucker** remains a staple in the lineup regardless of matchups, consistently delivering production against Pfaadt, while **Andy Pages** (65.76) adds depth in the outfield by capitalizing on his high-percentage base-stealing opportunities against a slower-delivering battery.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision today involved **Miguel Vargas**, who landed on the bench with a 52.17 efficiency score. Despite a strong xwOBA profile and a favorable lineup spot, **Miguel Vargas** ultimately lost out to the higher projected efficiency and immediate lineup alignment of our starting infielders. We continue to monitor the bench closely, as **Brayan Rocchio**, **Curtis Mead**, and **Jorge Polanco** remain ready, though their individual matchups against pitchers like Perez and Rivera created enough statistical friction to keep them sidelined for this rotation.\n\nCaution is advised regarding the weather at our catcher position, as **Drake Baldwin** (56.39) faces a 32% rain risk that could compromise his volume. While **Drake Baldwin** is currently slated to start, we will keep an eye on stadium conditions leading up to first pitch. Elsewhere, **Liam Hicks** (55.48) and **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** (59.87) round out the core, with **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** providing necessary stability despite facing a tough arm in Buehler. We remain aggressive in utilizing **Jacob Wilson**, **Coby Mayo**, and **Josh Jung** to round out the bottom of the order, aiming to maintain our competitive edge in SB efficiency while protecting our game caps.",
